+# Job Queue Integration for Notifications Service
+
+## Overview
+
+The notifications service can consume jobs without direct database access through two mechanisms:
+
+### 1. Event Streaming (Recommended for Real-Time)
+
+Jobs are published to a Redis pub/sub channel when enqueued:
+
+**Channel:** `jobs`
+**Message Format:**
+```json
+{
+  "type": "job.ready",
+  "jobId": "uuid",
+  "payload": "{json payload as string}"
+}
+```
+
+**Usage:**
+```typescript
+const redis = require("redis");
+const client = redis.createClient({ url: process.env.REDIS_URL });
+
+client.subscribe("jobs", (message) => {
+  const event = JSON.parse(message);
+  if (event.type === "job.ready") {
+    handleNotification(event.jobId, event.payload);
+  }
+});
+```
+
+**Advantages:**
+- Real-time notification delivery
+- No polling overhead
+- Natural pub/sub semantics
+
+### 2. HTTP Polling API (Fallback Option)
+
+An HTTP endpoint is available to pull the next ready job:
+
+**Endpoint:** `GET /api/jobs/next`
+**Response on success (200):**
+```json
+{
+  "id": "uuid",
+  "payload": "{json payload as string}"
+}
+```
+**Response on no jobs (204):** No content
+
+**Usage:**
+```typescript
+async function pollJobs() {
+  const response = await fetch("https://our-service/api/jobs/next");
+  if (response.status === 204) {
+    return null; // No jobs available
+  }
+  return response.json();
+}
+```
+
+**Advantages:**
+- Simpler to implement for teams without Redis access
+- Natural HTTP semantics
+- Works across any firewall
+
+**Note:** When using HTTP polling, implement exponential backoff to avoid overwhelming the API during high job volume periods.
+
+## Implementation Details
+
+- Jobs are published immediately after database insert (transactional)
+- The database remains the source of truth; events are ephemeral
+- Job payload should be treated as opaque by the notifications service
+- Failed notification sends should not block job processing
+
+## Setup
+
+The notifications service needs access to one of:
+- **Redis instance** (for event streaming): `REDIS_URL` environment variable configured by our ops team
+- **HTTP endpoint** (for polling): Network access to our service (already required for other integrations)
+
+Neither approach requires database credentials.
